  {
    "episode_id": 51126,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51126.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "Welcome to the American Banker Podcast. I'm Neil Hagerty reporting from Washington. As chairman of the House Financial Services Committee, Jeb Henserling has built a reputation as a conservative who has pushed aggressively to roll back Dodd-Frank and limit the government's role in housing finance. At times, the Republican from Texas has resisted compromise on legislation, referring to stick to his principles, which has drawn some criticism as some of his policy proposals were viewed as too extreme in the Senate. However, more recently, he has been more open to a given take, and many of the derogatory measures he has pushed were ultimately included in a bipartisan reg relief bill signed into law in May. As Henserling prepares to retire from Congress, he spoke with me about his accomplishments as chairman, some pending reg relief measures, and the changes he hopes to see to the current housing finance system. Chairman Hanzerling, good to have you with us. Good to be here. You've been in Congress since 2003 and you've been holding the gavel at the House Financial Services Committee for the past six years. As you're about to leave, what do you think is your biggest accomplishment?",
