  {
    "episode_id": 51126,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51126.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Well, nobody has brought me any proposals. If somebody wants to bring a proposal willing to do that, but this went through regular order in the house. It was negotiated. It was compromised. We have a house product. The Senate essentially told us, take it or leave it. We haven't quite sent the same message to the Senate, but I would say this. Everything in that bill, I repeat. Everything in Jobs 3.0 is strongly bipartisan. If somebody wishes to add a provision, I'm not saying no, but either A, it has to be strongly bipartisan for possibility of inclusion. If it's partisan, then it's going to have to be counterbalanced with something partisan on the other end. There are a lot of bills that I'm wondering to see in Jobs 3.0 that I dropped because they weren't strongly bipartisan. So, partisan measures are going to have to be counterbalanced by other partisan measures on the other side of the political spectrum. Otherwise, again, something that comes out 406 to 4, I would urge the Senate to take it up as is, just like we did with 2155. That is what I urge the Senate to do, but I have long since learned to I think quote the title of a James Bond movie, Never Say Never. So if they want to propose changes, that is their right. But again, they're going to have to be strongly bipartisan measures to be added to this package bill.",
