  {
    "episode_id": 51126,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51126.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "Well, again, I think we can do both. I think one, you have to fight for your vision, you have to fight for your principles, but that doesn't prevent you from acting incrementally. I think by just about any measure whatsoever, you will find the financial services committee to have been among the most productive, if not the most productive committee in Congress. When you measure us by the number of bills that we passed out of committee, bills passed on the floor, bills signed into law, number of hearings, number of markups. I think by that lens, by that measurement, you will find us an incredibly productive committee and you will also find most of our measures, not all. Most of our measures passed on a bipartisan basis, many on a strongly bipartisan basis. But simply because I work for incremental change doesn't mean that my principles have changed and it doesn't change what is right and what is wrong and what helps working men and women, what lifts people from property, what helps create modern economic growth, what helps people get into houses, what helps people start small businesses. So I think those are very, very important battles to have. And to me, capitalism is one of the great forces of good in the history of mankind. It is clearly, again, help people get into homes that otherwise would not help homes. It helps people have nutritional meals, who otherwise wouldn't have nutritional meals. It helps people start their own business, who otherwise would be an employment of others. It helps people sleep at night, not having to worry whether or not there's enough money tomorrow to buy groceries and to pay the drugist bill. I never want anybody to confuse capitalism with crony capitalism. because it confuses people. It besmirches the reputation and integrity of the greatest housing, nutritional, and education program devised by the mind of man, and that is free enterprise, a system that has never been enjoyed more robustly than in America. But I fear that subsidies and set aside in earmarks And special interests can undermine that system. And I think we see vestiges of it today. And at the end of the day, conservatives will never be able, never be able to reform welfare as long as our hands are unclean with respect to corporate welfare. and you cannot take America off the road to bankruptcy unless ultimately you reform current entitlement programs for future generations.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Capitalism is a powerful force for good in human history.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The financial services committee has been among the most productive committees in Congress.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Incremental action does not imply abandoning core principles.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Free enterprise has enabled access to housing, nutrition, entrepreneurship, and financial security.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Most of the measures passed by the financial services committee were bipartisan.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Crony capitalism distorts public perception of genuine capitalism.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Subsidies, earmarks, and special interests threaten the integrity of free enterprise.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Reforming corporate welfare is necessary to reform welfare overall.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Entitlement reforms are essential to avoid national bankruptcy.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Free enterprise is inherently beneficial and superior to state-controlled alternatives.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Productivity in Congress should be measured by legislative output and process metrics like bills passed and hearings held.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
