  {
    "episode_id": 51126,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51126.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Well, why not today? I mean, I can do it tomorrow. I can do it yesterday. I can do it today. So I'm getting ready to leave Congress, as you know, otherwise we wouldn't be having this interview. I would say if you had a couple of hours, I could go through my list of disappointments in serving in Congress for 16 years. Probably the biggest disappointment I have is that I was not able to get signed into law a comprehensive housing finance reform bill. I think a government guarantee in the secondary mortgage market is not necessary and it's very risky. Having fought it for a number of years, I sense that it is going to be a longstanding feature of our housing finance system. So if we're going to have it, and if there's going to be some kind of compromise, I'm worried about the status quo. I'm worried about the status quo because I think that risk or building up in the system yet again, I fear we are repeating many of the same mistakes that brought us to the crisis in the first place. So again, I don't want there to be a government guarantee, but if there is going to be one, We at least need elements of competition, innovation, risk dispersion, and put the taxpayer in a catastrophic loss position only, which is what I think you saw the work that has taken many, many months to get here to have, at least on the house side, a bipartisan, comprehensive bill. So I'm under no illusions that this is going to get signed into law in this Congress. It is my intention to take this as far as it can be taken in this Congress. And I'm hopeful that it will serve as a foundation if people will come to their senses and come to the bargaining table in the next Congress once I'm gone.",
