    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "That's a decision for the administration to make. Well, it's a decision for him to make whether or not he wants to resign. It's a decision that the administration has to make, whether or not he can be removed for cause. Again, any accusations of sexual harassment have to be taken very, very seriously. His accuser didn't just come with an accusation. She came with evidence. She had tapes. Having said that, Mao Watt deserves to have his defense. I think he could have been a little bit more forthcoming in our hearing. than he was, but again, ultimately a committee hearing is not going to be the art. The ultimate dispenser of justice or the ultimate trier of fact. Both of those parties deserve justice and due process, including Mel Watts. So based on that hearing, I'm not prepared to leap to the conclusion. that necessarily the accuser is correct, nor am I ready to necessarily leap to the conclusion that Mel Watt is without some culpability. But I do believe in this system that he's deserving of the presumption of innocence. So again, I don't get to determine whether Mel Watt resigns. I don't get to determine whether he is removed for cause. And I'm not sure all the facts have come out in our hearing. Some of the facts did and it's necessary for us because it is our job to have oversight in this. So I'm not prepared to call for his resignation, but I still think there are very serious issues that he is not addressed.",
